# Rank 1666 / CVE-2019-12041 (markdown-it prior art):
# a markdown parser's HTML-tag recognizer is assembled at module scope out of
# per-construct regex literals, and two of those literals carry an alternation
# of overlapping prefixes under a star: the HTML comment body
# `([^-]+|[-][^-]+)*` and the CDATA body
# `([^\]]+|\][^\]]|\]\][^>])*`. Each branch can end a run at the same character
# the next iteration can begin it, so a body that never reaches the closing
# delimiter is re-split at every boundary and the match cost grows without
# bound in the length of the input (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression
# complexity, CWE-400). The composed recognizer is applied to the document text
# at every inline position, so a crafted comment or CDATA section hangs the
# parse. The rewrite removes the ambiguity rather than capping the input: the
# comment body becomes `<!---->|<!--(?:-?[^>-])(?:-?[^-])*-->`, where every
# iteration must consume a non-dash and the closing delimiter is unreachable
# from inside the loop, and the CDATA body becomes the lazy `[\s\S]*?`, which
# cannot split a run at all.
#
# The module composes the literals through a local `replace` helper that folds
# each fragment's `.source` into a master pattern and compiles it once with
# `new RegExp`, so the literal and the untrusted operand never meet in one
# expression: the recognizer is exported and applied in a different file
# (`state.src.slice(pos).match(HTML_TAG_RE)` at lib/rules_inline/htmltag.js:37),
# where the pattern arrives as a variable and no regex literal exists.
#
# The pool row carries no flag and reads correctly: 287dfbf2 is the targeted
# two-pattern change inherited from markdown-it, the vulnerable literals are
# present at its parent, and the remediation is present at the commit itself.
# No pool correction is needed.
#
# Recheck measurements (3 x `--cache off --max-ram 6GB` per revision): both
# revisions report the same 33 findings and the same 5 review flags at the same
# locations, and neither `lib/common/html_re.js` nor `lib/rules_inline/
# htmltag.js` carries a finding on either side. The engine's shared classifier
# still discriminates the pair exactly -- the vulnerable revision lowers the
# two literals to synthesised `__regex.match` call nodes at html_re.js:44 and
# :47, the fixed revision synthesises none -- and still no JavaScript binding
# consumes them (Ruby turns the same node into `code.RegexMatch` and
# `code.CatastrophicRegex` through `regexMatch205` and `catastrophicRegex1701/
# 1703` in vyql/bindings/ruby/m5a48bf47.vyql; the JavaScript bindings carry
# only per-CVE literal fragments).
#
# Three throwaway probes (removed, not shipped) re-measure the presence route:
#
# 1. The ungated verdict -- `node.path ~= "__regex.match"` emitting
#    `code.CatastrophicRegex` -- discriminates the real trees 2 -> 0 and is
#    unshippable, as first measured across ~250 trees: moment +6, URI.js +4,
#    prompts.chat +3, qwik +1 over seven collateral trees with the probe
#    subtracted (mathjs and tinymce +0).
#
# 2. The shipped Ruby gate ported -- verdict plus a same-scope match-family
#    application (`node.context.callPath contains "__regex.match"` and
#    `containsAny(node.context.callPath, [".match"])`, the shape of
#    `catastrophicRegex1701/1703`) -- reports 0 on both revisions. The
#    declaring module never applies the pattern: the `.match` lives in another
#    file where no literal exists, so the gate that makes the Ruby form
#    shippable cannot see this code at all.
#
# 3. The one gate that does reach the pair -- verdict plus a same-scope
#    `RegExp` construction -- reports 1 -> 0 on the real trees and is still
#    unshippable, for two measured reasons. On a bundled file a module-scope
#    gate is whole-file co-membership: five of moment's six rows sit on the
#    shipped copies of one source file (moment.js, meteor/moment.js,
#    min/moment.min.js, min/moment-with-locales*.js) where an ambiguous literal
#    and a `new RegExp` thousands of lines apart satisfy it, and URI.js's other
#    three rows sit on vendored bundles (jquery-1.7.2.min.js, prettify.js,
#    src/URI.min.js). And the sixth moment row is src/lib/parse/regex.js --
#    the bounded `{0,256}`/`{1,256}` branches moment shipped as its own ReDoS
#    remediation -- because `jsRegexQuantifier` maps every `{...}` body except
#    `1` and `0,1` to `*`, so an explicitly bounded repeat classifies exactly
#    like an unbounded one. Minimal triple isolating that defect: the bounded
#    form `/[0-9]{0,256}['a-z...]{1,256}|[...]{1,256}(\s*?[...]{1,256}){1,2}/`
#    synthesises a `__regex.match` node, the identical `*`-spelled form
#    synthesises one, and the bound-free form synthesises none. Across eight
#    trees the gate adds 15 rows, of which two are defensible -- this pair's
#    own, and URI.js:231, `find_uri_expression`, a genuinely catastrophic URL
#    regex -- one real in seven, the ratio the ungated criterion already
#    measured, now at the gated level too.
#
# What would make this catchable is a taint gate, and only one endpoint of it
# exists: at the application file the exported rule's `state` parameter is
# labelled `code.ExternalEntryInput` by `library.param-source`
# (htmltag.js:16), so the untrusted half -- `state.src.slice(pos).match(...)` --
# is covered, but no sink exists for a match-family call whose pattern arrives
# as a variable: every shipped match-family sink keys on an argument literal
# (`catastrophicRegex543/546/548/549`, `regexMatch540`), and the ambiguity
# verdict lives at the literal in the other file. At the literal's file the
# pattern is a module-scope constant no untrusted value reaches, so a sink on
# the synthesised node can never receive taint there either. Linking the two
# needs the engine to propagate a composed regex constant's ambiguity across a
# module boundary into the call that applies it -- a dataflow relation that
# does not exist -- plus a boundedness-aware quantifier model so remediated
# `{n,m}` forms stop classifying. Both are engine work, not bindings; the
# narrower fragment form that would catch this one library names this fix's own
# pattern text and is the repro shape the genericity test excludes.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost, both revisions rejected.

test "remarkable: html recognizer folds ambiguous comment and cdata bodies into the tag matcher"
  lang javascript
  file lib/common/html_re.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  reject_review code.CatastrophicRegex
  code
  ```
  function replace(regex, options) {
    regex = regex.source;
    options = options || '';

    return function self(name, val) {
      if (!name) {
        return new RegExp(regex, options);
      }
      val = val.source || val;
      regex = regex.replace(name, val);
      return self;
    };
  }

  var close_tag = /<\/[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*\s*>/;
  var comment   = /<!--([^-]+|[-][^-]+)*-->/;
  var cdata     = /<!\[CDATA\[([^\]]+|\][^\]]|\]\][^>])*\]\]>/;

  var HTML_TAG_RE = replace(/^(?:close_tag|comment|cdata)/)
                      ('close_tag', close_tag)
                      ('comment', comment)
                      ('cdata', cdata)
                      ();

  module.exports.HTML_TAG_RE = HTML_TAG_RE;
  ```

test "remarkable: html recognizer with iteration-forced comment and lazy cdata bodies is clean"
  lang javascript
  file lib/common/html_re.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  reject_review code.CatastrophicRegex
  code
  ```
  function replace(regex, options) {
    regex = regex.source;
    options = options || '';

    return function self(name, val) {
      if (!name) {
        return new RegExp(regex, options);
      }
      val = val.source || val;
      regex = regex.replace(name, val);
      return self;
    };
  }

  var close_tag = /<\/[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*\s*>/;
  var comment   = /<!---->|<!--(?:-?[^>-])(?:-?[^-])*-->/;
  var cdata     = /<!\[CDATA\[[\s\S]*?\]\]>/;

  var HTML_TAG_RE = replace(/^(?:close_tag|comment|cdata)/)
                      ('close_tag', close_tag)
                      ('comment', comment)
                      ('cdata', cdata)
                      ();

  module.exports.HTML_TAG_RE = HTML_TAG_RE;
  ```
